13
00:00:46,650 --> 00:00:50,660
America's tariff threat. Story 1. America

14
00:00:50,710 --> 00:00:54,210
has proposed a 25%

15
00:00:54,260 --> 00:01:00,660
tariff on Indian goods. What is that law, and what can India do about it?

16
00:01:00,710 --> 00:01:04,990
On 25th November, the Office of the United States Trade

17
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:07,610
representative proposed a tariff

18
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:11,690
of up to 25% on goods from India.

19
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:14,510
Under section 301 of America's

20
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:17,150
trade Act of 1974

21
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:20,280
it says India's policies create

22
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:23,890
"structural excess capacity" in manufacturing,

23
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:26,430
which it calls unreasonable.

24
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:30,910
There is a twist if India and America sign a trade deal.

25
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:33,600
The rate is capped at 17%.

26
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:38,520
Nothing is in force yet. Public comments are open until January 5.

27
00:01:38,570 --> 00:01:43,150
And a hearing is set for January 25, 2027.

28
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:47,370
Why section 301 now? Because in February this year,

29
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:56,750
the United States Supreme Court struck down the earlier tariffs that were imposed under an emergency-powers law called

30
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:00,750
IEEPA. So Washington has moved

31
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:03,420
to an older, narrower tool.

32
00:02:03,470 --> 00:02:07,350
Goods already covered under a different law,

33
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:10,280
Section 232, like aluminium,

34
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:13,200
steel, vehicles and semiconductors.

35
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:19,190
Reported to be left out. For India, America is the biggest export market.

36
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:23,010
So this hits textiles, gems, engineering goods

37
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:28,930
and more. India has disputed the finding, and the real question for Mains is this:

38
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:32,720
Can a country call another country's industrial policy,

39
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:39,140
like make in India unfair and punish it without going to the World Trade Organization.

40
00:02:39,190 --> 00:02:43,600
Section 232 is run by the US Commerce Department and is about

41
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:47,970
National security. Do not mix them both. It is very important.

42
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:56,110
Your first question, how many of these statements are correct?

43
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:01,960
The answer is B.

44
00:03:02,010 --> 00:03:05,490
Only two. The tariff is only proposed.

45
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:11,640
Comments close on January 5th and the hearing is on January 25th.

46
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:15,250
Nepal's Prime Minister in Delhi.

47
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:18,110
Story 2 Nepal's Prime Minister

48
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:23,970
recently visited India and this is his first visit after he became Prime Minister.

49
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:27,400
And what are the two countries agree upon and sign upon?

50
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:32,500
On November 22nd and 23rd, Nepal's Prime Minister Balendra Shah,

51
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:39,350
popularly known as Balen, made his first state visit to India since taking office in March.

52
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:44,610
The two countries signed eight MoUs: on space, police and forensic training,

53
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:48,010
civil-service training, health, traditional medicine,

54
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:52,800
urban development and agriculture. India also announced help for

55
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:56,800
Nepal's post-flood rebuilding and energy projects,

56
00:03:56,850 --> 00:04:02,600
reported at about 15,000 crore rupees:

57
00:04:02,650 --> 00:04:05,300
3,500 crore as grants

58
00:04:05,350 --> 00:04:09,600
and 11,500 crore as a line of credit.

59
00:04:09,650 --> 00:04:16,710
Now, the static base, our relationship rests on the Treaty of Peace and Friendship of 1950.

60
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:21,120
Which gives an open border and lets Nepali citizens

61
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:25,870
live and work in India. The two rivers we share also have treaties:

62
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:32,440
The Kosi in 1954, the Gandak in 1959 and the Mahakali in 1996.

63
00:04:32,490 --> 00:04:37,710
Which plans the Pancheshwar project. And there is a sore point:

64
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:40,460
The Kalapani, Lipulekh

65
00:04:40,510 --> 00:04:42,960
and Limpiyadhura area,

66
00:04:43,010 --> 00:04:48,660
which Nepal showed inside its own map in 2020.

67
00:04:48,710 --> 00:04:52,860
Prelims trap. Nepal touches five Indian states.

68
00:04:52,910 --> 00:04:57,070
Uttarakhand, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, West Bengal and Sikkim.

69
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:00,330
So students often forget Sikkim and get confused.

70
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:04,150
So question 2, how many of these Indian states

71
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:10,230
share a border with Nepal.

72
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:16,100
The answer is B.

73
00:05:16,150 --> 00:05:19,000
Only three: Uttarakhand, Sikkim and West Bengal.

74
00:05:19,050 --> 00:05:21,760
Himachal Pradesh does not touch Nepal.

75
00:05:21,810 --> 00:05:25,680
Story 3: an extension to last week's current affairs.

76
00:05:25,730 --> 00:05:29,330
It ended with no deal at all. What survives?

77
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:33,190
Last week we told you the climate summit in Antalya

78
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:38,010
had a draft that dropped the fossil-fuel language. Here is the update.

79
00:05:38,060 --> 00:05:42,160
On November 22nd, COP31 ended without

80
00:05:42,210 --> 00:05:44,930
adopting the draft, the "Antalya Pact",

81
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:50,510
at all. The European Union, Britain and several small island countries

82
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:54,650
refused a text with no mention of fossil fuels,

83
00:05:54,700 --> 00:05:58,180
while oil producers refused to add it.

84
00:05:58,230 --> 00:06:08,300
This is the first time since Copenhagen in 2009 that a climate summit has closed without an overall political agreement.

85
00:06:08,350 --> 00:06:15,030
So what survives? The Paris Agreement is untouched and the Dubai decision of 2023.

86
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:17,910
To transition away from fossil fuels.

87
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:22,260
Still stands. Smaller technical decisions were also adopted.

88
00:06:22,310 --> 00:06:25,610
For India, the stand stays the same:

89
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:29,370
Common but differentiated responsibilities

90
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:32,300
and finance from the developed world first.

91
00:06:32,350 --> 00:06:39,420
Question 3, which of these is correct?

92
00:06:39,470 --> 00:06:46,680
The answer is B: 2 and 3 only.

93
00:06:46,730 --> 00:06:49,500
The Antalya Pact was not adopted.

94
00:06:49,550 --> 00:06:52,010
India is Asia's

95
00:06:52,060 --> 00:06:55,820
third power. Story 4: India has been ranked

96
00:06:55,870 --> 00:06:59,280
third in Asia and who gives this ranking?

97
00:06:59,330 --> 00:07:03,620
Let us decode.

98
00:07:03,670 --> 00:07:08,910
On November 22nd, Australia's Lowy Institute released its Asia Power Index for 2026.

99
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:14,140
India kept the third place in Asia, behind the United States and China.

100
00:07:14,190 --> 00:07:16,880
With a reported score of about

101
00:07:16,930 --> 00:07:22,550
40.5 out of 100. The index ranks 27 countries on 8 measures.

102
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:26,030
Economic capability, military capability, resilience.

103
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:28,730
Future resources, economic relationships,

104
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:32,900
Defence networks, diplomatic influence and cultural influence.

105
00:07:32,950 --> 00:07:36,300
India is strong on future resources and resilience.

106
00:07:36,350 --> 00:07:40,330
But weaker on economic relationships and defence networks.

107
00:07:40,380 --> 00:07:45,370
Prelims trap: the Asia Power Index is published by the Lowy Institute,

108
00:07:45,420 --> 00:07:50,010
a think tank in Sydney, not by the United Nations or the World Bank.

109
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:57,340
Question 4. Consider these statements.

110
00:07:57,390 --> 00:08:03,680
The answer is A, only one.

111
00:08:03,730 --> 00:08:07,460
The World Happiness Report comes from the United Nations

112
00:08:07,510 --> 00:08:11,250
sustainable Development Solutions Network and Partners

113
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:16,860
and the Global Hunger Index from Concern Worldwide and Welthungerhilfe.

114
00:08:16,910 --> 00:08:20,950
Pakistan's first astronaut. Story 5:

115
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,880
Pakistan has sent its first astronaut

116
00:08:23,930 --> 00:08:29,950
to the Chinese space station. So let us see what our position is.

117
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,510
On November 25th,

118
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:35,990
China launched Shenzhou-24 from Jiuquan

119
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:39,730
on the Long March 2F rocket.

120
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:45,100
On board was Muhammad Zeeshan Ali, Pakistan's first astronaut,

121
00:08:45,150 --> 00:08:47,610
flying as a payload specialist.

122
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:50,310
He became the first foreign astronaut.

123
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:53,860
To visit China's own space station.

124
00:08:53,910 --> 00:08:59,460
Why does it matter? Tiangong is China's own space station,

125
00:08:59,510 --> 00:09:02,190
outside the International Space Station.

126
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:06,870
So, it shows China building its own space partnership.

127
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:09,590
And this one is with our neighbour.

128
00:09:09,640 --> 00:09:15,630
Now line up India's record. Rakesh Sharma flew in 1984 on a Soviet Soyuz mission.

129
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:21,310
In 2025, Shubhanshu Shukla became the first Indian on the International Space Station on

130
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:28,180
the Axiom-4 mission. And India's own Gaganyaan programme plans its first uncrewed test flight,

131
00:09:28,230 --> 00:09:31,550
with a Bharatiya Antariksh Station planned by 2035.

132
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:37,140
China is not a partner of the International Space Station.

133
00:09:37,190 --> 00:09:40,490
Its station is Tiangong. And here is a place trap:

134
00:09:40,540 --> 00:09:45,350
The Jiuquan launch centre is named after a city in Gansu,

135
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:49,340
but it actually sits in Inner Mongolia.

136
00:09:49,390 --> 00:09:57,320
Question 5, how many of these statements are correct?

137
00:09:57,370 --> 00:10:04,040
The answer is B only two.

138
00:10:04,090 --> 00:10:07,090
Tiangong is China's own

139
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:08,310
station.

140
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:16,660
Five big stories done. Ikkada varaku unte, meeru serious aspirant: you are really preparing for the examination.

141
00:10:16,710 --> 00:10:21,550
We have done the world part; now we come to our own home. We will discuss our own

142
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:26,030
rivers, our own bridges and also we will be discussing about

143
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:29,370
our own cities. Strait of Hormuz.

144
00:10:29,420 --> 00:10:34,190
Story 6: tankers in the Strait of Hormuz have been burning.

145
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:41,550
Why should India worry? And what is the link between the Strait of Hormuz and our Visakhapatnam?

146
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:45,140
This month Iran's Revolutionary Guard said,

147
00:10:45,190 --> 00:10:50,420
several oil tankers struck sea mines in the Strait of Hormuz and caught fire.

148
00:10:50,470 --> 00:10:53,670
These claims could not be independently verified.

149
00:10:53,720 --> 00:10:57,530
Traffic through the Strait of Hormuz has dropped sharply,

150
00:10:57,580 --> 00:11:02,100
and the Indian Navy has been reported escorting India's tankers through the area.

151
00:11:02,150 --> 00:11:04,920
Why does one narrow strait matter so much?

152
00:11:04,970 --> 00:11:09,680
The Strait of Hormuz lies between Iran in the north and Oman's

153
00:11:09,730 --> 00:11:12,770
Musandam peninsula in the south, and joins

154
00:11:12,820 --> 00:11:16,470
the Persian Gulf to the Gulf of Oman and the Arabian Sea.

155
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:19,770
About a fifth of the world's oil passes through it.

156
00:11:19,820 --> 00:11:25,220
And India imports nearly 85% of its crude oil.

157
00:11:25,270 --> 00:11:29,780
Now the Telugu States Lens. India keeps an emergency oil stock

158
00:11:29,830 --> 00:11:34,540
in underground rock caverns, and the very first of them

159
00:11:34,590 --> 00:11:37,880
is in our Visakhapatnam.

160
00:11:37,930 --> 00:11:42,450
The other two are at Mangaluru and Padur in Karnataka.

161
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:46,730
For a total of 5.33 million tonnes,

162
00:11:46,780 --> 00:11:50,990
run by Indian Strategic Petroleum Reserves Limited.

163
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:54,860
Prelims trap: Hormuz connects the Persian Gulf

164
00:11:54,910 --> 00:11:58,790
to the Gulf of Oman; Bab-el-Mandeb connects

165
00:11:58,840 --> 00:12:04,350
the Red Sea to the Gulf of Aden; and Malacca connects the Indian Ocean

166
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:12,950
to the south China Sea. How many pairs are correctly matched?

167
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:19,720
The answer is B, only two.

168
00:12:19,770 --> 00:12:23,830
Bab-el-Mandeb joins the Red Sea to the Gulf of Aden;

169
00:12:23,880 --> 00:12:27,100
the Suez Canal joins it to the Mediterranean.

170
00:12:27,150 --> 00:12:30,330
Made-in-India machines. Story 7:

171
00:12:30,380 --> 00:12:34,630
The Cabinet has cleared 11,000 crore rupees

172
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:37,570
to manufacture big machines in our country.

173
00:12:37,620 --> 00:12:40,620
And why now? On November 25th,

174
00:12:40,670 --> 00:12:45,100
the Union Cabinet approved an 11,000-crore-rupee scheme

175
00:12:45,150 --> 00:12:48,880
to make construction and infrastructure equipment in India.

176
00:12:48,930 --> 00:12:52,660
Tunnel boring machines, ship-to-shore cranes, hydraulic

177
00:12:52,710 --> 00:12:55,990
excavators and heavy off-road trucks.

178
00:12:56,040 --> 00:13:01,870
The Ministry of Heavy Industries will run it for eight years from 2026-27.

179
00:13:01,920 --> 00:13:04,690
Makers get an incentive linked to their

180
00:13:04,740 --> 00:13:09,420
extra sales each year and up to 375 crore rupees will help.

181
00:13:09,470 --> 00:13:18,410
Set up a national testing facility. Why now? India's construction-equipment industry is already the third largest in the world, after China and America,

182
00:13:18,460 --> 00:13:21,340
but we import many of the biggest machines,

183
00:13:21,390 --> 00:13:27,660
mostly from China. China has also started asking for export licences for tunnel boring machines,

184
00:13:27,710 --> 00:13:30,780
the machines that dig our metro and highway tunnels.

185
00:13:30,830 --> 00:13:34,390
Prelims trap: the scheme is run by the Ministry of Heavy Industries,

186
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:38,740
and not by Ministry of Commerce and its incentive is tied to

187
00:13:38,790 --> 00:13:44,390
incremental sales, the same idea as the Production Linked Incentive schemes.

188
00:13:44,440 --> 00:13:48,530
Question 7: which ministry implements the new

189
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:54,020
construction equipment scheme.

190
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:02,490
The answer is B, the Ministry of Heavy Industries.

191
00:14:02,540 --> 00:14:06,230
Nitish Setu: India now has its

192
00:14:06,280 --> 00:14:08,730
Longest river bridge.

193
00:14:08,780 --> 00:14:12,250
And now let us line up all such river bridges.

194
00:14:12,300 --> 00:14:13,460
In one place.

195
00:14:13,510 --> 00:14:19,810
On November 21st, the Prime Minister opened Nitish Setu in Bihar,

196
00:14:19,860 --> 00:14:22,310
a six-lane bridge over the Ganga

197
00:14:22,360 --> 00:14:25,770
that is 9.76 kilometers long.

198
00:14:25,820 --> 00:14:29,480
It joins Kachchi Dargah near Patna

199
00:14:29,530 --> 00:14:34,300
with Bidupur in Vaishali district, and with its approach roads the

200
00:14:34,350 --> 00:14:38,780
corridor runs to about 19.76 kilometres.

201
00:14:38,830 --> 00:14:44,070
It is reported to be India's longest river bridge. So, let us line up the bridge records.

202
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:47,340
Because examiners love look-alikes.

203
00:14:47,390 --> 00:14:50,650
Bhupen Hazarika Setu over the Lohit in Assam,

204
00:14:50,700 --> 00:14:53,230
about 9.15 kilometres,

205
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:55,830
was the longest river bridge until now.

206
00:14:55,880 --> 00:14:58,630
Bogibeel over the Brahmaputra near

207
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:03,210
Dibrugarh is India's longest rail-cum-road bridge.

208
00:15:03,260 --> 00:15:08,660
Atal Setu in Mumbai, about 21.8 kilometres, is India's

209
00:15:08,710 --> 00:15:16,650
Longest sea bridge, and Mahatma Gandhi Setu between Patna and Hajipur held the river record from

210
00:15:16,700 --> 00:15:19,710
1982 to 2017

211
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:26,500
Question 8. How many pairs are correctly matched?

212
00:15:26,550 --> 00:15:33,110
The answer is B only 2.

213
00:15:33,160 --> 00:15:36,800
Atal Setu crosses the sea at Mumbai, not the Hooghly.

214
00:15:36,850 --> 00:15:40,690
Constitution Day: November 26th. We Indians

215
00:15:40,740 --> 00:15:43,330
celebrate and remember Constitution Day,

216
00:15:43,380 --> 00:15:48,090
but was the Constitution enforced or adopted on that day?

217
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:52,600
Constitution Day, Milk Day and 26/11. November 26th was Constitution Day,

218
00:15:52,650 --> 00:16:01,630
or Samvidhan Divas, observed since 2015. It marks the day in 1949 when the Constituent Assembly adopted our Constitution.

219
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:04,570
This year was the 77th anniversary.

220
00:16:04,620 --> 00:16:09,250
The full constitution came into force on January 26, 1950.

221
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:12,760
But some articles like those on citizenship and elections.

222
00:16:12,810 --> 00:16:16,600
Started on November 26th, 1949 itself.

223
00:16:16,650 --> 00:16:23,170
The same day is National Milk Day, the birthday of Dr Verghese Kurien, born in 1921,

224
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:26,530
the father of the White Revolution who led Operation Flood.

225
00:16:26,580 --> 00:16:29,940
And built the Amul Cooperative Model at Anand.

226
00:16:29,990 --> 00:16:36,090
And November 26th also marks 18 years since the 2008 Mumbai terror attacks.

227
00:16:36,140 --> 00:16:42,510
After that attack, India created the National Investigation Agency through

228
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:46,470
the NIA Act of 2008 and strengthen its

229
00:16:46,520 --> 00:16:49,680
coastal security.

230
00:16:49,730 --> 00:16:58,440
Question 9. Consider these statements.

231
00:16:58,490 --> 00:17:04,240
The answer is B.

232
00:17:04,290 --> 00:17:06,970
One and three only. Two heroes,

233
00:17:07,020 --> 00:17:10,590
two real heroes of November 24th.

234
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:14,430
Story 10: two important personalities.

235
00:17:14,480 --> 00:17:17,590
A general who beat the Mughals on a river,

236
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:20,780
and a Sikh Guru who gave his life.

237
00:17:20,830 --> 00:17:23,090
For freedom of faith.

238
00:17:23,140 --> 00:17:26,320
On November 24, Lachit Divas

239
00:17:26,370 --> 00:17:29,980
the 404th birth anniversary of Lachit

240
00:17:30,030 --> 00:17:34,250
Borphukan, the Ahom general born in 1622.

241
00:17:34,300 --> 00:17:39,950
In 1671, he defeated a much larger Mughal army in the Battle of Saraighat,

242
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,440
a battle fought on the Brahmaputra near Guwahati.

243
00:17:43,490 --> 00:17:47,780
